    <h1>Isaiah Hartwell</h1>
    <p>Author &amp; founder, Made Out of Clay Productions</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p>Isaiah Hartwell writes picture books for the kids who feel things deeply — the patient ones, the loud-world-overwhelmed ones, the ones who see the world a little differently and make it more beautiful for it.</p>

    <p>Made Out of Clay Productions began with a father creating the affirming stories he wanted his autistic son to have: stories where a child can be fully known, never reduced to a diagnosis or a lesson. <a href="/blog/why-it-is-called-made-out-of-clay/">Read the full founding story.</a></p>

    <p>That belief grew into four print series:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>At Sunny's Table</strong> — a gentle sloth family learning patience, quiet, and belonging.</li>
      <li><strong>Just the Way You Are</strong> — identity-first stories of neurodiversity and inclusion, inspired by a real boy named Clayton.</li>
      <li><strong>The Kingdom of Judea</strong> — Caleb and the Cubs on an epic lion-kingdom adventure about courage and home.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/series/titus-the-lamb/">Titus the Lamb</a></strong> — sing-along Bible stories built around one big question and one memorable song.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Nine titles are available in premium full-color, 8.5×8.5 hardcover editions. <em>Titus and the Lions</em> is coming soon, with a free read-along preview available now.</p>

    <p>The free Book Club also includes <strong>Brave Like Me</strong>, a digital-only collection for ages {{ site.digitalAgeRange }}. It is not counted as a fifth print series.</p>

    <h2>How the books are made</h2>
    <p>Isaiah Hartwell is a pen name—the storytelling voice of Made Out of Clay, a small family-run studio. Modern AI tools assist parts of drafting and illustration, but they do not make publishing decisions or approve finished books.</p>
    <ol class="process-list">
      <li><strong>Concept and purpose:</strong> a human chooses the child, feeling, or family moment the story should serve.</li>
      <li><strong>Drafting and revision:</strong> the studio develops the manuscript, revises its voice, and removes generic or unsupported language.</li>
      <li><strong>Illustration direction:</strong> every scene is directed and reviewed for character, setting, tone, and visual continuity.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial review:</strong> text and images are checked together for clarity, respectful language, age fit, and series consistency.</li>
      <li><strong>Proofing and print proof:</strong> layout, spelling, color, trim, and the physical edition are reviewed before final approval.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Where a story addresses neurodiversity, the studio uses identity-first, deficit-free language and avoids presenting a child's differences as something to cure. We do not name or publish private details about the child who inspired the imprint beyond what the family has intentionally chosen to share.</p>

